We now need to implement the next core QA Copilot capability:

USER PASTES A WEBSITE URL → SYSTEM ANALYZES THE WEBSITE → AI GENERATES TEST CASES → USER REVIEWS → APPROVES → TEST CASES ARE SAVED.

IMPORTANT:
This is for a prototype showcase tomorrow.

DO NOT:
- redesign the existing Test Cases page
- replace the existing AI architecture
- rewrite working test-case CRUD
- remove the existing requirement-based AI generator
- install unnecessary dependencies
- build a production-grade web crawler
- implement authentication crawling
- implement CAPTCHA bypass
- implement unrestricted crawling
- modify unrelated modules

Reuse the existing Test Cases page, AI provider/service, test-case API, flow store and UI components.

IMPLEMENTATION:

1. Update the existing "Generate with AI" flow.

Add source options:

- Website URL
- Requirement / User Story
- API Endpoint

Keep the existing Requirement/User Story generation working.

2. For Website URL:

Show:

Website URL input
Framework selector
Test type selector
Number of scenarios

Example:

URL:
https://example.com

Framework:
Playwright

Test Type:
Functional

3. Add backend endpoint:

POST /api/ai/analyze-url

Input:

{
  "url": "https://example.com"
}

Create a safe prototype URL-analysis service.

For the prototype, do NOT build a generic production crawler.

The analyzer should return a normalized structure such as:

{
  "url": "...",
  "title": "...",
  "pages": [],
  "forms": [],
  "actions": [],
  "interactiveElements": []
}

If real URL inspection is unreliable or blocked, use a deterministic prototype analysis based on the supplied URL and clearly keep the implementation provider-agnostic.

Do not fabricate that a real browser crawl occurred.

4. Add/extend the AI generation endpoint so URL analysis can be passed into the existing AI service.

The AI generation input should contain:

- website URL
- discovered pages
- forms
- actions
- interactive elements
- selected framework
- selected test type
- requested scenario count

Generate structured test cases containing:

- title
- type
- priority
- framework
- confidence
- preconditions
- steps
- expected result
- tags

5. Frontend flow:

Generate with AI
→ Website URL
→ Analyze Website
→ show analysis progress
→ show Website Analysis Summary
→ Generate Test Cases
→ show generated test cases
→ allow selecting/rejecting/editing
→ Approve Selected
→ save approved test cases through the existing test-case API
→ return/show them in the existing Test Cases table

6. Website Analysis Summary UI:

Show:

Pages discovered
Forms detected
Interactive elements
Potential test areas

Example:

Website Analysis Complete

Pages: 4
Forms: 3
Interactive Elements: 18
Test Areas: 8

7. Generated test cases should use the existing TestCase type/schema wherever possible.

Do NOT create a duplicate test-case model.

8. Example generated scenarios for a login page should include:

- Valid login
- Invalid password
- Invalid email
- Empty email
- Empty password
- Forgot password
- Unauthorized access
- Security/negative scenario where appropriate

9. Preserve the existing dark QA Copilot visual design.

10. Keep the existing mock AI provider as the default for demo reliability.

11. Gemini support must continue to work through the existing provider abstraction.

12. Add proper loading, error and empty states.

13. Do not break:
- existing test-case CRUD
- existing requirement-based generation
- test data
- automation
- test runs
- failure analysis

14. Run:
- frontend typecheck
- backend typecheck
- ESLint
- production build

At the end, report:
- files changed
- endpoints added/changed
- user flow implemented
- validation results
- any limitations

Do not modify anything outside the scope of this feature.